= Carbon cycle =

== Overview ==
The '''carbon cycle''' consists of the processes by which carbon moves among Earth's atmosphere, oceans, land biosphere, soils and geological reservoirs. Carbon is conserved through the underlying physical and chemical transformations, but the amounts stored in each reservoir can change, and human activity can disrupt formerly approximate balances.

== Reservoirs and fluxes ==
A '''reservoir''' is a stock of carbon in a defined part of the Earth system. A '''flux''' is the rate at which carbon moves from one reservoir to another. The ocean, plants, soils, atmosphere and carbonate rocks hold different carbon stocks and exchange them over different timescales. A large natural flux is not by itself evidence that anthropogenic additions are negligible; what matters for accumulation is the net imbalance among fluxes and reservoirs.

== Fast and slow pathways ==
In the relatively fast biological cycle, [[Related to::Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]] fixes atmospheric or dissolved carbon dioxide into organic molecules. Respiration, decomposition and combustion can return carbon dioxide to the surrounding environment. Marine exchange and biological processes also move carbon among the surface ocean, organisms and deeper waters.

The slow geological cycle includes weathering, sedimentation, burial, rock formation, tectonic processes and volcanic return of carbon. Some of these transfers operate over millions of years. Their rates and mechanisms are not interchangeable with annual atmospheric carbon balances.

== Human-driven perturbations ==
Fossil-fuel combustion and land-use change move additional carbon into the active atmosphere–ocean–biosphere system. A portion accumulates in the atmosphere; other portions are absorbed by land and ocean sinks. Ocean carbon dioxide uptake also contributes to [[Related to::Ocean acidification|ocean acidification]], changing marine carbonate chemistry.

== Relation to climate ==
Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas, so changes in atmospheric abundance can affect Earth's energy balance. The carbon cycle is connected to [[Related to::Climate change|climate change]], but a stock-and-flux diagram alone does not prove a particular temperature response; radiative physics and observations are also needed.

== Models and measurement ==
A reservoir model should state its boundaries, units, period, sampling method and uncertainties. Carbon and carbon dioxide masses are not numerically interchangeable: one metric ton of carbon corresponds to about 3.67 metric tons of carbon dioxide by molecular mass. Long-term and annual flux values should not be compared without consistent units and dates.

== Measurement and stock–flow models ==
The carbon cycle is a physical and biochemical set of transfers; the claim that it can be '''represented by a state model''' does not mean the cycle itself executes that model. In a stock–flow representation, reservoirs define quantities of carbon, while exchanges define rates. For a chosen reservoir, change over time is the sum of incoming fluxes minus outgoing fluxes. Such a balance is a bookkeeping identity until supported by observations and a model of the processes controlling fluxes.

Estimation requires the observational source, spatial and temporal boundary, the units of carbon mass versus carbon-dioxide mass, and the uncertainty of exchange rates. A computational model can test hypotheses about poorly observed flows or reconstruct time-varying reservoir states, but estimated quantities and direct measurements must retain distinct provenance.

[[Related to::Photosynthesis|Photosynthesis]] includes biochemical regulation and signaling at cellular scales. Those mechanisms are distinct from the processes represented by global carbon stock–flow models. See [[Scientific model]] and [[Measurement]].

